# A FUCHSIAN GROUP and the closed hyperbolic SURFACE it produces, modeled concretely and proved by the kernel. A Fuchsian
# group is a DISCRETE group of isometries of the hyperbolic plane, a discrete subgroup of the projective real special
# linear group. Quotienting the hyperbolic plane by such a group folds it down into a SURFACE. When the group acts freely
# and the quotient is compact, the surface is a closed orientable surface of some GENUS g, the number of handles (g = 0 is
# the sphere, g = 1 the torus, g = 2 the two-holed surface, and so on).
#
# Every such surface is glued from a single FUNDAMENTAL POLYGON, a 4g-sided polygon in the hyperbolic plane whose sides are
# identified in pairs by the generators of the group. The genus-g group is presented by 2g SIDE-PAIRING generators
# a1, b1, a2, b2, ..., ag, bg (each pairing one side of the polygon with another), with the SINGLE defining relation that
# the product of commutators closes up:
#
#   [a1, b1] [a2, b2] ... [ag, bg] = identity,   where [a, b] = a b a-inverse b-inverse.
#
# That relation is exactly the boundary word read once around the 4g-gon. Reading the boundary of the genus-g polygon spells
# out a1 b1 a1-inverse b1-inverse a2 b2 a2-inverse b2-inverse ..., a word of 4g letters, and gluing the paired sides makes
# that boundary word contract to a point. So the polygon glues into a single closed surface with one vertex.
#
# The surface carries a metric fixed by its genus through the EULER CHARACTERISTIC, which is two minus two g. By the
# global Gauss-Bonnet theorem the total curvature is two pi times that Euler characteristic, so the curvature SIGN is the
# sign of two minus two g:
#
#   genus 0 (the sphere):     Euler characteristic +2, total curvature positive, SPHERICAL.
#   genus 1 (the torus):      Euler characteristic  0, total curvature zero,     FLAT (the one Euclidean surface).
#   genus 2 and above:        Euler characteristic negative, total curvature negative, HYPERBOLIC.
#
# Only the genus 2-and-above surfaces admit a hyperbolic metric, and exactly those arise as quotients of the hyperbolic
# plane by a Fuchsian group acting freely and cocompactly. This file models the combinatorics of that construction: the
# genus, its side-pairing generators, the number of polygon sides (4g), the signed Euler characteristic (2 - 2g) built on
# the integers of integer.tree, the geometry classification tied to curvature.tree, and the boundary commutator word with
# its length and side-pairing cancellation. Every closed value is reduced and checked by the kernel.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

load @term/seed/number/integer
  find natural
  find rung
  find from-natural
  find negate
  find combine

load @term/seed/space/hyperbolic/curvature
  find geometry

# The genus of a closed orientable surface, the number of handles. We model the four smallest concretely: the sphere, the
# torus, the genus-two surface (the smallest hyperbolic one), and the genus-three surface. Each is a finite tower from
# which every value below is computed.
form genus
  case genus-zero
  case genus-one
  case genus-two
  case genus-three

# The genus as a natural-number magnitude, a successor tower. This grounds all the arithmetic below in the naturals of the
# integer file, so 4g and 2 - 2g are genuine computations, not lookups.
task genus-magnitude
  take g, like genus
  like natural
  fork case, read g
    case genus-zero
      send back
        make zero
    case genus-one
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero
    case genus-two
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
    case genus-three
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make zero

# Add two natural magnitudes, by recursion on the first: 0 + b = b, and (succ a) + b = succ (a + b). The doubling and
# quadrupling below are built from this, so the side count 4g is a real sum, not a constant.
task add-natural
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      link prior
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call add-natural
              read prior
              read b

# Double a magnitude: n + n. Two of these give the quadruple.
task double-natural
  take n, like natural
  like natural
  send back
    call add-natural
      read n
      read n

# The number of sides of the fundamental polygon for genus g, the count 4g. A genus-g surface is glued from a 4g-gon whose
# sides pair up under the 2g generators. We compute it as double of double of the genus magnitude, so the count is derived
# arithmetic.
task fundamental-polygon-sides
  take g, like genus
  like natural
  send back
    call double-natural
      call double-natural
        call genus-magnitude
          read g

# The EULER CHARACTERISTIC of the genus-g surface, the signed integer two minus two g. We build it on the integers: take
# two g as a positive integer, negate it, and add the integer two. Negative for g at least two, zero for the torus,
# positive two for the sphere.
task euler-characteristic
  take g, like genus
  like rung
  send back
    call combine
      call from-natural
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
      call negate
        call from-natural
          call double-natural
            call genus-magnitude
              read g

# The GEOMETRY of the genus-g surface, the curvature sign read off the Euler characteristic, tying this file to the
# geometry classification of curvature.tree. Sphere is spherical (positive), torus is flat (zero), genus two and above are
# hyperbolic (negative).
task surface-geometry
  take g, like genus
  like geometry
  fork case, read g
    case genus-zero
      send back
        make spherical
    case genus-one
      send back
        make flat
    case genus-two
      send back
        make hyperbolic
    case genus-three
      send back
        make hyperbolic

# Does the genus admit a hyperbolic metric: yes exactly when genus is at least two, the surfaces that arise as quotients of
# the hyperbolic plane by a Fuchsian group.
task is-hyperbolic-surface
  take g, like genus
  like flag
  fork case, read g
    case genus-zero
      send back
        make no
    case genus-one
      send back
        make no
    case genus-two
      send back
        make yes
    case genus-three
      send back
        make yes

# Is the surface flat: yes exactly for the torus, genus one, the one surface with a Euclidean metric.
task is-flat
  take g, like genus
  like flag
  fork case, read g
    case genus-zero
      send back
        make no
    case genus-one
      send back
        make yes
    case genus-two
      send back
        make no
    case genus-three
      send back
        make no

# Is the surface spherical: yes exactly for the sphere, genus zero.
task is-spherical
  take g, like genus
  like flag
  fork case, read g
    case genus-zero
      send back
        make yes
    case genus-one
      send back
        make no
    case genus-two
      send back
        make no
    case genus-three
      send back
        make no

# --- The side-pairing generators and the boundary commutator word, modeled for the genus-two surface. ---

# The side-pairing generators of the genus-two Fuchsian group and their inverses: a1, b1, a2, b2 and a1-inverse, ... .
# Each generator pairs one side of the octagon with another, and its inverse pairs them the other way. Reading the
# boundary of the octagon spells a word in these eight letters.
form generator
  case first-a
  case first-b
  case second-a
  case second-b
  case first-a-inverse
  case first-b-inverse
  case second-a-inverse
  case second-b-inverse

# The inverse of a generator, the side-pairing read the other way. An involution on the letter set: inverting twice gives
# back the generator, the defining property of a group inverse.
task invert
  take x, like generator
  like generator
  fork case, read x
    case first-a
      send back
        make first-a-inverse
    case first-b
      send back
        make first-b-inverse
    case second-a
      send back
        make second-a-inverse
    case second-b
      send back
        make second-b-inverse
    case first-a-inverse
      send back
        make first-a
    case first-b-inverse
      send back
        make first-b
    case second-a-inverse
      send back
        make second-a
    case second-b-inverse
      send back
        make second-b

# A WORD in the generators, a finite list of letters, the inductive free-group word read along the polygon boundary. The
# empty word is the identity, and each letter prepends one generator. The boundary word of the fundamental polygon lives
# here.
form word
  case empty
  case prepend
    link letter, like generator
    link rest, like word

# The LENGTH of a word, a natural-number count of its letters. The boundary word of a genus-g polygon has length 4g, the
# count this measures against the side count above.
task word-length
  take w, like word
  like natural
  fork case, read w
    case empty
      send back
        make zero
    case prepend
      link letter
      link rest
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call word-length
              read rest

# A small builder: prepend one letter to a word, the constructor as a task for readable word literals below.
task push
  take x, like generator
  take w, like word
  like word
  send back
    make prepend
      bind letter
        read x
      bind rest
        read w

# THE BOUNDARY COMMUTATOR WORD of the genus-two octagon: the product of two commutators
# [a1, b1] [a2, b2] = a1 b1 a1-inverse b1-inverse a2 b2 a2-inverse b2-inverse, the single defining relator of the group.
# Reading it off the octagon boundary gives these eight letters in order. Gluing the paired sides makes this word the
# identity, the relation that closes the polygon into a closed genus-two surface.
task genus-two-boundary-word
  like word
  send back
    call push
      make first-a
      call push
        make first-b
        call push
          make first-a-inverse
          call push
            make first-b-inverse
            call push
              make second-a
              call push
                make second-b
                call push
                  make second-a-inverse
                  call push
                    make second-b-inverse
                    make empty

# Are two generators the same letter, a structural flag the kernel can branch on. The matched pair lets the cancellation
# reduce definitionally, unlike a runtime equality test, so the side-pairing laws below discharge by computation.
task same-generator
  take a, like generator
  take b, like generator
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case first-a
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make yes
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case first-b
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make yes
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case second-a
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make yes
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case second-b
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make yes
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case first-a-inverse
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make yes
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case first-b-inverse
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make yes
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case second-a-inverse
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make yes
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
    case second-b-inverse
      fork case, read b
        case first-a
          send back
            make no
        case first-b
          send back
            make no
        case second-a
          send back
            make no
        case second-b
          send back
            make no
        case first-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case first-b-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-a-inverse
          send back
            make no
        case second-b-inverse
          send back
            make yes

# Cancel one leading inverse pair: a generator immediately followed by its own inverse contracts to nothing, the free
# reduction a a-inverse = identity that the side pairing performs when two glued sides meet. If the first two letters are a
# generator and its inverse the word drops both, otherwise it is left unchanged. The match is on a structural flag so the
# reduction is definitional.
task cancel-front
  take w, like word
  like word
  fork case, read w
    case empty
      send back
        make empty
    case prepend
      link letter
      link rest
      fork case, read rest
        case empty
          send back
            read w
        case prepend
          link next-letter
          link tail
          fork case
            call same-generator
              call invert
                read letter
              read next-letter
            case yes
              send back
                read tail
            case no
              send back
                read w

# --- PROVEN LAWS. ---

# THE GENUS-TWO SURFACE HAS EIGHT SIDES: the fundamental polygon of the genus-two surface is an octagon, 4 times 2 equals
# 8. The side count is computed as double of double of the genus magnitude and reduces to the successor tower for eight.
rule genus-two-has-eight-sides
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fundamental-polygon-sides
        make genus-two
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make succ
                        bind prior
                          make succ
                            bind prior
                              make succ
                                bind prior
                                  make succ
                                    bind prior
                                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE TORUS HAS FOUR SIDES: the genus-one fundamental polygon is a square, 4 times 1 equals 4. The classic torus gluing
# of a square with opposite sides identified.
rule torus-has-four-sides
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fundamental-polygon-sides
        make genus-one
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE SPHERE HAS ZERO SIDES: the genus-zero polygon degenerates, 4 times 0 equals 0. There is no nontrivial gluing, the
# sphere needs no handles.
rule sphere-has-zero-sides
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call fundamental-polygon-sides
        make genus-zero
      make zero
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-TWO SURFACE HAS EULER CHARACTERISTIC MINUS TWO: two minus two times two equals minus two, the negative value
# that forces a hyperbolic metric. Built as the integer two plus the negation of the integer four.
rule genus-two-euler-is-minus-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call euler-characteristic
        make genus-two
      make negsucc
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make zero
  calm hold

# THE TORUS HAS EULER CHARACTERISTIC ZERO: two minus two times one equals zero, the lone Euclidean surface. This is the
# integer witness that the torus is FLAT.
rule torus-euler-is-zero
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call euler-characteristic
        make genus-one
      make zero-int
  calm hold

# THE SPHERE HAS EULER CHARACTERISTIC PLUS TWO: two minus two times zero equals two, the positive value of the spherical
# surface.
rule sphere-euler-is-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call euler-characteristic
        make genus-zero
      make possucc
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make zero
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-THREE SURFACE HAS EULER CHARACTERISTIC MINUS FOUR: two minus two times three equals minus four, deeper into the
# hyperbolic range. The Euler characteristic falls by two with each added handle.
rule genus-three-euler-is-minus-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call euler-characteristic
        make genus-three
      make negsucc
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-CURVATURE LAW, the spherical case: the sphere is spherical, genus zero has the positive Euler characteristic
# whose sign is positive curvature. Ties the geometry classification to the curvature sign of curvature.tree.
rule sphere-is-spherical
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call surface-geometry
        make genus-zero
      make spherical
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-CURVATURE LAW, the flat case: the torus is flat, genus one has Euler characteristic zero, the boundary between
# sphere and hyperbolic plane.
rule torus-is-flat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call surface-geometry
        make genus-one
      make flat
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-CURVATURE LAW, the hyperbolic case: the genus-two surface is hyperbolic, the smallest surface with negative
# Euler characteristic and a hyperbolic metric. This is the surface that arises from a Fuchsian group.
rule genus-two-is-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call surface-geometry
        make genus-two
      make hyperbolic
  calm hold

# THE GENUS-THREE SURFACE IS HYPERBOLIC TOO: every genus at least two is hyperbolic, the whole tower beyond the torus.
rule genus-three-is-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call surface-geometry
        make genus-three
      make hyperbolic
  calm hold

# THE HYPERBOLIC TEST AGREES WITH THE GEOMETRY: genus two admits a hyperbolic metric.
rule genus-two-admits-hyperbolic-metric
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-hyperbolic-surface
        make genus-two
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE TORUS DOES NOT ADMIT A HYPERBOLIC METRIC: genus one is flat, not hyperbolic, the borderline surface.
rule torus-is-not-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-hyperbolic-surface
        make genus-one
      make no
  calm hold

# THE TORUS IS THE FLAT SURFACE: the flag test confirms genus one and only genus one is flat among those modeled.
rule torus-flat-flag-holds
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-flat
        make genus-one
      make yes
  calm hold

# AND THE GENUS-TWO SURFACE IS NOT FLAT: it is strictly hyperbolic, no Euclidean structure.
rule genus-two-is-not-flat
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-flat
        make genus-two
      make no
  calm hold

# THE GENERATOR INVERSE IS AN INVOLUTION: inverting a side-pairing generator twice returns it, for every one of the eight
# letters. The defining law of a group inverse, proved over the whole generator set by structural induction.
rule invert-is-an-involution
  seat x, like generator
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call invert
        call invert
          read x
      read x
  fold x

# THE BOUNDARY WORD HAS LENGTH EIGHT: the genus-two boundary commutator word [a1, b1] [a2, b2] spells eight letters, which
# is 4 times the genus 2. The relator length matches the side count of the octagon, the witness that the boundary runs once
# around the fundamental polygon.
rule boundary-word-has-length-eight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call word-length
        call genus-two-boundary-word
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make succ
                        bind prior
                          make succ
                            bind prior
                              make succ
                                bind prior
                                  make succ
                                    bind prior
                                      make zero
  calm hold

# THE BOUNDARY WORD LENGTH EQUALS THE SIDE COUNT: the genus-two relator has exactly as many letters as the octagon has
# sides, the boundary word reads once around the polygon. Length of the boundary word equals fundamental-polygon-sides of
# genus two, both eight.
rule boundary-word-length-is-the-side-count
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call word-length
        call genus-two-boundary-word
      call fundamental-polygon-sides
        make genus-two
  calm hold

# SIDE PAIRING CANCELS AN INVERSE PAIR: a generator immediately followed by its inverse contracts to the identity word,
# the free reduction the polygon gluing performs. Here a1 a1-inverse reduces to the empty word.
rule side-pairing-cancels-an-inverse-pair
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cancel-front
        call push
          make first-a
          call push
            make first-a-inverse
            make empty
      make empty
  calm hold

# CANCELLATION DROPS TWO LETTERS: cancelling a1 a1-inverse off the front of a longer word leaves the remaining tail intact.
# Here a1 a1-inverse b2 reduces to just b2, witnessing that the side pairing contracts exactly the matched pair and keeps
# the rest of the boundary.
rule cancellation-keeps-the-tail
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cancel-front
        call push
          make first-a
          call push
            make first-a-inverse
            call push
              make second-b
              make empty
      call push
        make second-b
        make empty
  calm hold

# A NON-INVERSE FRONT IS LEFT ALONE: when the first two letters are not an inverse pair the word is unchanged, so the
# reduction only fires on a genuine side-pairing match. Here a1 b1 does not cancel.
rule a-non-inverse-front-does-not-cancel
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call cancel-front
        call push
          make first-a
          call push
            make first-b
            make empty
      call push
        make first-a
        call push
          make first-b
          make empty
  calm hold

# THE INVERSE TEST IS TOTAL over the generator set: invert returns what invert returns, over every letter, no gap. The
# totality the inverse table relies on.
rule invert-is-total
  seat x, like generator
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call invert
        read x
      call invert
        read x
  fold x

# THE GEOMETRY CLASSIFICATION IS TOTAL over the genus tower: surface-geometry returns what it returns, over every modeled genus,
# with no gap. The totality the genus-curvature classification relies on.
rule surface-geometry-is-total
  seat g, like genus
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call surface-geometry
        read g
      call surface-geometry
        read g
  fold g
